From 0f261f5641a042e75d6805ccdd3c53fa20cc8aae Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: dgabehar <dgabehar@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 23 Sep 2026 16:24:58 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] tests: fix stale expected output in the shorthand-class
 regexp test

"regexp with unsupported shorthand" used \w as its example of an
unsupported PCRE shorthand class, asserting the whole pattern falls
back to a generic string acceptor. That assumption predates
get_shorthand_class() in json-schema-to-grammar.cpp, which now fully
supports \d/\D/\w/\W/\s/\S as composable grammar tokens (see that
function's own comment) -- \w in "^[0-9]{3}\w$" is no longer
unsupported at all, so the actual output is now a correct, fully
expanded per-character grammar, not the stale expected fallback. This
was an intentional behavior improvement from an earlier patch that
never got this pre-existing test's expectation updated to match,
causing a hard assert(false) abort on every CI run (test-json-schema-
to-grammar.cpp:39) rather than a soft mismatch.

Fixed by swapping \w for \b (word-boundary assertion): a zero-width
construct with no GBNF equivalent, confirmed still genuinely
unsupported (gbnf_escape_length() doesn't recognize it, so it falls
through to unsupported_pattern). This keeps testing what the test's
own comment says it's for -- that a partial conversion's intermediate
rule doesn't leak into the grammar when the pattern is abandoned and
the whole thing falls back to the generic string acceptor -- without
asserting something the converter can actually do correctly today.

Found and fixed incidentally while investigating unrelated CI failures
on PR #5 (K2-Horizon EOG/reasoning-tag fixes) -- reproduced locally
(exit 134, SIGABRT) before making any change, confirmed unrelated to
that PR's 2 files by content inspection.

Assisted-by: Claude Sonnet
---
 tests/test-json-schema-to-grammar.cpp | 10 ++++++++--
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/tests/test-json-schema-to-grammar.cpp b/tests/test-json-schema-to-grammar.cpp
index d5873c19d0..6a745629ca 100755
--- a/tests/test-json-schema-to-grammar.cpp
+++ b/tests/test-json-schema-to-grammar.cpp
@@ -1460,13 +1460,19 @@ static void test_all(const std::string & title, std::function<void(const TestCas
         )"""
     });
 
-    // the rules of the partial conversion (here "root-0") must not leak into the grammar
+    // \w/\d/\s (and their negations) are now fully supported shorthand classes (see
+    // get_shorthand_class() in json-schema-to-grammar.cpp) -- \b (word-boundary
+    // assertion, a zero-width construct with no GBNF equivalent) is not, so it's
+    // used here instead to keep exercising this case: the rules of the partial
+    // conversion (here "root-0"/"root-1") must not leak into the grammar when the
+    // pattern is abandoned partway through and the whole thing falls back to the
+    // generic string acceptor.
     test({
         SUCCESS,
         "regexp with unsupported shorthand",
         R"""({
             "type": "string",
-            "pattern": "^[0-9]{3}\\w$"
+            "pattern": "^[0-9]{3}\\b$"
         })""",
         R"""(
             char ::= [^"\\\x7F\x00-\x1F] | [\\] (["\\bfnrt] | "u" [0-9a-fA-F]{4})
-- 
